%YAML 1.1
%TAG !u! tag:unity3d.com,2011:
--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7c7c947f0ccabb24dbf991df8e9f6d4f, type: 3}
  m_Name: rational-theories oc counsellin
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  defaultQuestions:
  - QuestionText: 'Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT) was developed by:'
    Options:
    - Aaron Beck
    - Albert Ellis
    - Carl Rogers
    - William Glasser
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The central premise of REBT is that emotional disturbances are
      primarily caused by:'
    Options:
    - Unconscious conflicts
    - Chemical imbalances in the brain
    - A lack of unconditional positive regard
    - Irrational beliefs about activating events
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The ABC model in REBT stands for:'
    Options:
    - Antecedent, Behavior, Consequence
    - Activating event, Belief, Emotional/Behavioral Consequence
    - Assessment, Belief, Change
    - Action, Behavior, Cognition
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'According to Ellis, the core of most psychological problems is:'
    Options:
    - The activating event itself
    - The absolutistic and dogmatic "musts," "shoulds," and "oughts"
    - The emotional consequence
    - The lack of social interest
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: Which of the following is NOT one of the three core "musts" identified
      by Ellis that lead to irrational thinking?
    Options:
    - '"I must do well and win approval."'
    - '"Others must treat me fairly and kindly."'
    - '"The world must give me happiness."'
    - '"I must understand my unconscious motivations."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'In REBT, the process of vigorously debating and challenging a
      client''s irrational beliefs is called:'
    Options:
    - Cognitive restructuring
    - Disputation
    - Systematic desensitization
    - Reflection
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The ultimate goal of REBT is to help clients replace their irrational
      beliefs with:'
    Options:
    - More rational, flexible, and non-dogmatic preferences
    - A complete absence of negative emotions
    - Perfectly logical and emotionless thinking
    - A dependence on the therapist's guidance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A student thinks, "I must get an A on this exam. It would be awful
      if I didn''t, and I would be a complete failure." This statement exemplifies:'
    Options:
    - Conditional self-acceptance
    - Awfulizing and self-downing
    - Low frustration tolerance
    - All of the above
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: Which technique in REBT involves having the client imagine the
      worst-case scenario of their fear and then realize they could still cope with
      it?
    Options:
    - Shame-attacking exercises
    - Rational-emotive imagery
    - Catastrophizing
    - Aversive conditioning
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'Ellis considered REBT to be a form of:'
    Options:
    - Psychodynamic therapy
    - Cognitive-behavioral therapy
    - Humanistic-existential therapy
    - Pure behaviorism
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher using REBT principles with a perfectionist student would
      help them challenge the belief that:'
    Options:
    - '"It is preferable to do well."'
    - '"I can tolerate frustration."'
    - '"Sometimes failure is a learning opportunity."'
    - '"I must be perfect to be a worthwhile person."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "Low Frustration Tolerance" (LFT) is characterized
      by beliefs such as:'
    Options:
    - '"This situation is challenging, but I can handle it."'
    - '"The world must be fair and I can''t stand it when it''s not!"'
    - '"I am a good person even when I make mistakes."'
    - '"I can learn from difficult experiences."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'In the ABC model, the ''C'' (Consequence) refers to:'
    Options:
    - Only the behavioral response
    - Only the emotional response
    - Both the emotional and behavioral responses
    - The activating event itself
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A key difference between Ellis''s REBT and Beck''s Cognitive Therapy
      is that REBT is:'
    Options:
    - More passive and non-directive
    - More philosophical, confrontational, and focuses on "musts"
    - Less concerned with behavioral techniques
    - More focused on the therapeutic relationship
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A student who is rejected by a friend and thinks, "This is terrible!
      I can''t stand being alone! No one will ever like me!" is demonstrating:'
    Options:
    - Awfulizing, Low Frustration Tolerance, and Overgeneralization
    - Realistic and adaptive thinking
    - Healthy emotional processing
    - Mindfulness and acceptance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The ''D'' and ''E'' in the extended ABCDE model stand for:'
    Options:
    - Diagnosis and Evaluation
    - Disputation and New Effect
    - Dream Analysis and Empathy
    - Directive and Empathic
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'According to REBT, unhealthy negative emotions (like depression
      and rage) are primarily caused by:'
    Options:
    - Activating events
    - Irrational beliefs
    - The therapist's style
    - Genetic predispositions
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A technique where clients are encouraged to act in a "shameful"
      way in public to challenge their irrational fears of disapproval is called:'
    Options:
    - Aversive therapy
    - Shame-attacking exercises
    - Flooding
    - Systematic desensitization
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The role of the REBT therapist is best described as that of a(n):'
    Options:
    - Blank slate
    - Unconditional supporter
    - Teacher, coach, and persuader
    - Dream interpreter
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The philosophical foundation of REBT is most closely aligned with:'
    Options:
    - Stoicism (e.g., "People are disturbed not by things, but by the views they
      take of them.")
    - Existentialism
    - Structuralism
    - Determinism
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A student believes, "It''s absolutely necessary that I be popular,
      and it''s horrible if I''m not." An REBT counselor would help them develop
      a more rational belief such as:'
    Options:
    - '"It''s not important to have friends."'
    - '"I am a total loser if I''m not popular."'
    - "\"I would\_prefer\_to be popular, but it's not a dire necessity. I can still
      accept myself.\""
    - '"Everyone must like me."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'Critically analyzing REBT, a potential limitation in a school
      setting is that its confrontational style:'
    Options:
    - May not be suitable for all students, particularly younger or more sensitive
      ones
    - Is not evidence-based
    - Is too passive for adolescents
    - Focuses too much on the past
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "Unconditional Self-Acceptance" (USA) in REBT involves:'
    Options:
    - Rating one's behaviors but not one's global self
    - Believing one is a wonderful person only when successful
    - Avoiding all self-evaluation
    - Seeking constant external validation
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher using REBT with a student who has intense test anxiety
      would focus on disputing the student''s belief that:'
    Options:
    - '"Exams are challenging."'
    - '"I must not feel anxious."'
    - "\"It would be\_awful\_to fail, and I\_couldn't stand it.\""
    - '"I need to prepare for the test."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'Compared to Person-Centered Therapy, REBT is significantly more:'
    Options:
    - Non-directive
    - Directive and didactic
    - Focused on the unconscious
    - Ambiguous in its goals
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The "E" in the ABCDE model (New Effect) refers to:'
    Options:
    - The new, more functional emotional and behavioral consequences
    - The end of therapy
    - The environmental context
    - The empathic response of the therapist
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'Ellis argued that to achieve psychological health, individuals
      must work toward:'
    Options:
    - The complete elimination of negative emotions
    - High frustration tolerance and unconditional self-acceptance
    - Perfect relationships with everyone
    - Understanding their childhood traumas
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A student who procrastinates might hold the irrational belief:'
    Options:
    - '"This task is difficult, so I''ll start with a small part."'
    - '"I should do this now because it''s important."'
    - "\"This assignment is too hard, and I\_shouldn't\_have to do things that are
      hard!\""
    - '"I can ask for help if I need it."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'REBT is considered "behavioral" because it:'
    Options:
    - Ignores behavior completely
    - Uses behavioral techniques and emphasizes acting against irrational beliefs
    - Focuses only on animal learning
    - Is identical to radical behaviorism
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A core philosophical idea in REBT is that humans have a secondary,
      innate tendency to:'
    Options:
    - Think rationally and actualize themselves
    - Suppress all unconscious material
    - Remain emotionally neutral
    - Think irrationally and disturb themselves
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A school counselor using REBT with a bullied student would help
      them dispute the idea that:'
    Options:
    - '"The bullying is wrong and should be reported."'
    - '"Because they bully me, I am a worthless person."'
    - '"I feel hurt and angry when I am bullied."'
    - '"I can seek support from trusted adults."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The term "awfulizing" refers to:'
    Options:
    - Viewing a negative event as highly undesirable
    - A technique used in systematic desensitization
    - Having a realistic assessment of risk
    - Catastrophizing and believing a situation is 100% bad than it should be
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'In REBT, homework assignments are crucial because they:'
    Options:
    - Keep the client busy between sessions
    - Allow the client to practice identifying and disputing irrational beliefs in
      real life
    - Are used to test the client's intelligence
    - Replace the need for therapy sessions
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A parent using REBT principles would respond to a child''s failure
      by saying:'
    Options:
    - '"You are a failure and a bad kid."'
    - '"This outcome is terrible and unbearable!"'
    - '"You failed this time. Let''s look at what we can learn from it. You are still
      a worthwhile person."'
    - Nothing, to avoid reinforcing the failure.
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: Ellis originally trained in and practiced which form of therapy
      before developing REBT?
    Options:
    - Person-Centered Therapy
    - Psychoanalysis
    - Behavior Therapy
    - Gestalt Therapy
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The concept of "Unconditional Other-Acceptance" (UOA) in REBT
      involves:'
    Options:
    - Approving of all others' behaviors
    - Refusing to rate others' global worth, even when their behavior is objectionable
    - Believing others must always treat you well
    - Seeking constant approval from others
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A strength of REBT in educational settings is its emphasis on:'
    Options:
    - Long-term, open-ended exploration
    - Focusing solely on emotional expression
    - Avoiding any direct confrontation
    - Teaching students to take responsibility for their own emotional responses
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A student thinks, "My teacher didn''t call on me. She must hate
      me." An REBT practitioner would identify the core irrational process here as:'
    Options:
    - Mind reading and personalization
    - Realistic assessment
    - High frustration tolerance
    - Unconditional self-acceptance
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The main goal of Disputation (D) in the ABCDE model is to:'
    Options:
    - Agree with the client's irrational beliefs
    - Help the client see the logical, empirical, and pragmatic flaws in their irrational
      beliefs
    - Analyze the client's dreams
    - Provide unconditional support
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'REBT makes a distinction between healthy and unhealthy negative
      emotions. For example, the healthy alternative to anxiety is:'
    Options:
    - Apathy
    - Shame
    - Euphoria
    - Concern
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A teacher who feels intense rage when a student disrupts the class
      might be holding the irrational belief:'
    Options:
    - '"I don''t like this disruption."'
    - "\"This student\_should not\_behave this way, and they are a\_rotten person\_for
      doing so!\""
    - '"I will implement the classroom consequences."'
    - '"The student is probably having a bad day."'
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'REBT is considered a pioneering form of Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy
      because it:'
    Options:
    - Was the first to explicitly integrate thinking and behavior as core to emotional
      change
    - Rejected the role of cognition entirely
    - Focused only on behavioral activation
    - Was developed after Beck's Cognitive Therapy
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A student with social anxiety might be assigned the homework of
      intentionally saying something slightly foolish in a group to learn that the
      consequences are not "awful." This is an example of:'
    Options:
    - A shame-attacking exercise
    - Aversive conditioning
    - Free association
    - Transference analysis
    CorrectOptionIndex: 0
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The ultimate purpose of REBT''s forceful and persistent style
      is to:'
    Options:
    - Humiliate the client into changing
    - Help clients deeply internalize a new, rational philosophy of life
    - Make the therapist feel powerful
    - Quickly suppress symptoms without long-term change
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A limitation of REBT is that it may:'
    Options:
    - Be too superficially focused on symptoms
    - Underemphasize the therapeutic alliance for some clients
    - Be too long-term and expensive
    - Ignore the role of cognition completely
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'In REBT, "Self-Downing" refers to:'
    Options:
    - Giving oneself positive affirmations
    - The process of disputing irrational beliefs
    - A technique for building self-esteem
    - Rating one's entire self or essence as bad or worthless based on specific behaviors
    CorrectOptionIndex: 3
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'A school counselor using an eclectic approach might use REBT techniques
      specifically to:'
    Options:
    - Explore a student's early childhood memories in depth
    - Quickly challenge and change a student's debilitating performance anxiety before
      an exam
    - Provide non-directive support for a grieving student
    - Interpret a student's dreams
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'Ellis believed that profound philosophical change requires:'
    Options:
    - Insight alone
    - Repeated cognitive, emotive, and behavioral practice
    - A single powerful interpretation
    - Medication
    CorrectOptionIndex: 1
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
  - QuestionText: 'The "B" in the ABC model (Belief) can be:'
    Options:
    - Only irrational
    - Only rational
    - Either rational or irrational
    - Neither rational nor irrational
    CorrectOptionIndex: 2
    LevelName: rational-theories oc counsellin
